  "verdict": "The directed-opportunity design and emitted/delivered schedule are suitable for the first native CI increment. An exact-retry identity check is absent from the current generic trace checker; concrete counterexample communicated to the author. Native implementation remains to be reviewed.",
  "validated": [
    {
      "id": "common_directed_capacity",
      "finding": "One frame per directed opportunity makes idle-peer control responses consume the same resource as baseline control carriers. Receiving only queues replies; the terminal drain cannot transmit them."
    },
    {
      "id": "admission_accounting",
      "finding": "Offered, committed, delivered, and uncommitted request counters prevent budget-induced blocking from being reported as recovery."
    },
    {
      "id": "common_capture",
      "finding": "Before opportunity 1 is an exogenous common cut. The intended complete current session-state capture and native no-pending-entropy assertion avoid silently comparing native root-only disclosure against pending baseline private keys."
    },
    {
      "id": "network_accounting",
      "finding": "Loss/reorder/duplicate fate is indexed by opportunity, not protocol counter. verify_trace correctly ignores unoccupied slots and checks exact source frame bytes."
    },
    {
      "id": "physical_persistence",
      "finding": "The contract separates native journal commit/sync operations from reference returned/serialized states and leaves absent reference physical I/O as null."
    },
    {
      "id": "spqr_encapsulation_state",
      "finding": "The upstream encapsulation-side Ct1Sampled state retains es, but encaps1 has already returned EpochSecret and lib send has already added that epoch to the captured chain. The existing pending_dk-only recovery path therefore does not omit a separate not-yet-installed epoch for these branches."
    }
  ],
